{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The problem asks what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001 answers neither half. Its claim narrows the task class to composite-constraint problems and its mechanism explains why a panel prompted with a checklist *could* miss fewer conditions, but it never identifies any evidentiary signal that would separate a panel whose reliability stems from genuine understanding of each constraint from a panel that merely mechanically ticks boxes and coincidentally aligns with the verifiable outcome. The two counterconditions (holistic-synthesis tasks, checklist fatigue) address boundary threats to the claim, not the requested discriminator. A single evaluation showing high panel accuracy and high checklist completion is precisely ambiguous between genuine gain and coincidental compliance, and SRC_001 gives no criterion — e.g., outcome-calibration on perturbed constraint variants, error-structure analysis, or a decoy/inconsistent constraint probe — to resolve that ambiguity. That is a direct omission of the problem's core ask.","counterexample":null}]}